>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <div class="mb-12">
        <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-brand-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-3">Blog</p>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
          Construction Project Closeout Checklist
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">
          A step-by-step closeout checklist for specialty subcontractors — punch list, closeout
          documents, AIA paperwork, final pay application, lien waivers, and retainage.
        </p>
        <AuthorByline />
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-400">
          Published August 18, 2026 · Updated September 8, 2026 · 12 min read
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <p>
          The physical work is done. The last pipe is in the ground, the last panel is wired, the
          last tile is set. But the money isn't in your account yet — and it won't be until closeout
          is finished. For subcontractors, closeout is where retained funds either arrive or sit,
          sometimes for months, waiting on a missing O&amp;M manual or an unsigned lien waiver from
          a supplier.
        </p>
        <p>
          This construction project closeout checklist is written for specialty trades, not for the
          GC assembling the owner turnover set. It covers project completion and closeout in the
          order that actually unsticks payment: punch list, closeout documents, change orders, the
          final pay application, and follow-through on retainage. (You will also see it searched as
          a close out checklist or close-out checklist — same work, same package.) The paperwork it
          references is free to download: a
          <a href="/templates/punch-list/">punch list template</a> to work the list down, a
          <a href="/templates/certificate-of-completion/"
            >certificate of substantial completion template</a
          > for the completion date and retainage release, and a
          <a href="/templates/submittal-log/">submittal log</a> to track closeout submittals.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 p-6 not-prose my-8">
          <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-3">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>
              Closeout starts before the punch list — collect O&amp;M manuals and mark as-builts
              while the work is being installed, not after.
            </li>
            <li>
              Project completion (substantial, then final) is not the same as closeout. Final
              payment follows the accepted closeout package, not the last day on site.
            </li>
            <li>
              A single missing closeout submittal — an O&amp;M manual, a supplier lien waiver, an
              AIA affidavit — can hold your entire retainage.
            </li>
            <li>
              Track every closeout item as a task with an owner and a deadline, not as a mental
              note.
            </li>
            <li>
              Submit your final pay application promptly after final completion; retainage release
              follows the billing, not the physical work.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>The construction project closeout checklist</h2>
        <p>
          Use this as the working closeout checklist. Check your subcontract and the project spec —
          usually Division 01 — for extras your trade owes, then assign each line an owner and a
          date inside the project.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 p-6 not-prose my-8">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">
          During construction
        </h3>
        <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-decimal pl-5 mb-6">
          <li>Markup as-builts as you install — reroutes, dimensions, equipment locations.</li>
          <li>File O&amp;M manuals and warranties at delivery, not at demobilization.</li>
          <li>Photograph concealed work before it is covered.</li>
          <li>
            Keep daily
            <a href="/features/site-logs/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline">site logs</a>
            and inspection records so the closeout package has a contemporaneous trail.
          </li>
          <li>
            Price and approve every
            <a href="/features/change-orders/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
              >change order</a
            >
            before extra work proceeds.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">
          Before the GC punch walk
        </h3>
        <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-decimal pl-5 mb-6" start="6">
          <li>Walk your own scope and write an internal punch list.</li>
          <li>Correct self-found items and photograph the completed work.</li>
          <li>Confirm sub-tier and supplier invoices are current.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">
          Closeout documents
        </h3>
        <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-decimal pl-5 mb-6" start="9">
          <li>As-built / record drawings for your scope.</li>
          <li>O&amp;M manuals for equipment you installed.</li>
          <li>Warranties and product data sheets; note warranty start dates.</li>
          <li>Inspection certificates and final permit sign-offs for your trade.</li>
          <li>Attic stock or spare parts if the spec requires them.</li>
          <li>Training or commissioning records, when you installed equipment that needs them.</li>
          <li>
            AIA closeout documents as required (G704 copy for your remaining items; G706 / G706A /
            G707 equivalents or waivers the GC collects from subs).
          </li>
        </ol>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-slate-900 uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">
          Financial closeout
        </h3>
        <ol class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-decimal pl-5" start="16">
          <li>Resolve outstanding change orders — additions and credits.</li>
          <li>Collect final lien waivers from sub-tiers and suppliers.</li>
          <li>
            Submit the final
            <a href="/features/pay-applications/" class="text-brand-600 hover:underline"
              >pay application</a
            >
            for remaining contract balance plus retainage.
          </li>
          <li>Execute your final lien waiver per contract (conditional until funds clear).</li>
          <li>Follow up until retainage actually lands in your account.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <h2>What construction project closeout involves</h2>
        <p>
          Closeout is the set of steps required after the physical work is complete to hand the job
          over to the owner and receive final payment. For a subcontractor the main categories are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Punch list completion</strong> — correcting every deficient or incomplete item before
            the GC accepts the work.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Closeout submittals</strong> — as-built drawings, warranties, O&amp;M manuals, inspection
            certificates, and any AIA affidavits the GC requires.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Final change orders</strong> — any outstanding scope changes documented and approved
            before the contract closes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Final pay application</strong> — the last progress billing that brings the contract
            to 100% complete and bills retainage.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Final lien waivers</strong> — unconditional waivers from you and any sub-tier subs
            or suppliers.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Retainage collection</strong> — following up until the withheld funds actually arrive.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The sequence matters. GCs typically won't process the final pay application until closeout
          submittals are accepted, and owners won't release retainage to the GC — and therefore to
          you — until the GC's closeout package is complete.
        </p>

        <h2>Project completion and closeout</h2>
        <p>
          Search and contracts both mix these terms, but they are not the same milestone. Project
          completion is about whether the work can be used. Closeout is about whether you get paid
          for it.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Substantial completion</strong> — the owner can occupy or use the work for its intended
            purpose. Punch list items may remain. The date often starts warranties and can stop liquidated
            damages. Documented with a Certificate of Substantial Completion.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Final completion</strong> — every punch item in your scope is corrected and accepted.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Closeout</strong> — documents accepted, waivers exchanged, final pay application processed,
            retainage released. This is project-management closeout: the contract is administratively
            finished, not just physically finished.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Treating substantial completion as the finish line is how retainage sits. The closeout
          checklist below the punch list is what moves the last dollars.
        </p>

        <h2>Before substantial completion</h2>

        <h3>Run your own punch list walk</h3>
        <p>
          Before the GC's official inspection, walk your own scope with a critical eye. Finding and
          correcting deficiencies yourself is faster and cheaper than working from someone else's
          list after the fact. Our
          <a href="/blog/punch-list-management-for-subcontractors/">punch list management guide</a> covers
          how to structure that internal walk and how to dispute items that fall outside your scope.
        </p>

        <h3>Closeout documents checklist</h3>
        <p>
          Your subcontract and the spec spell out the closeout documents construction teams expect
          from each trade. Collect them into one package — do not drip PDFs through email over three
          weeks. Common submittals:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>As-built drawings</strong> — the contract drawings marked up to show actual installed
            conditions: pipe routes changed in the field, equipment relocated, dimensions that differed
            from the design. The earlier you start marking these during construction, the less work you
            have at closeout.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Operations and maintenance (O&amp;M) manuals</strong> — manufacturer documentation
            for equipment you installed. Request these at delivery and file them while the job is live;
            chasing a vendor for a manual six months later is slow.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Product data and warranties</strong> — manufacturer warranties for materials and equipment,
            often required in the project specification. Note warranty start dates — many run from substantial
            completion, not from installation.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Inspection certificates and permits</strong> — any final inspections required for
            your scope (fire alarm, plumbing, electrical) must be signed off before closeout is complete.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Attic stock, spare parts, and keys</strong> — if Division 01 or your spec calls for
            extra filters, devices, or attic stock, missing that box will hold the package even when the
            work is perfect.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Training and commissioning records</strong> — for equipment you started up, the GC
            often needs sign-in sheets or test reports in the turnover set.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You do not issue the Certificate of Occupancy — that is an AHJ / owner item — but a
          missing inspection on your trade can still block occupancy, which blocks the GC's
          closeout, which blocks your retainage. Track permit finals the same way you track O&amp;M
          manuals.
        </p>

        <h3>AIA closeout documents</h3>
        <p>
          On jobs using AIA contract documents, the GC's closeout set usually includes more than the
          G704. Subcontractors are rarely the ones filling the official owner forms, but you will be
          asked for the backup that lets the GC sign them:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>AIA G704 — Certificate of Substantial Completion.</strong> Make sure the attached
            list of remaining work identifies your scope accurately. The date on this form often starts
            warranties.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Final G702 / G703 pay application.</strong> Line items to 100%, retainage billed.
            See our
            <a href="/blog/aia-pay-application-guide/">AIA pay application guide</a> for the math.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>AIA G706 and G706A</strong> — contractor affidavits of payment of debts and claims,
            and of release of liens. GCs typically collect matching lien waivers and affidavits from each
            sub and supplier before they can sign.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>AIA G707 — Consent of Surety to Final Payment</strong> — required when the work is
            bonded. If you carried a <a href="/blog/bid-bonds-vs-performance-bonds/"
              >performance or payment bond</a
            > on the subcontract, ask the surety what they need well before the last pay app.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          BuildWorkPro produces AIA-style progress billing; it does not generate licensed AIA
          Contract Documents. If the GC requires the copyrighted forms, transfer the same numbers
          and attach your waivers.
        </p>

        <h3>Resolve all outstanding change orders</h3>
        <p>
          Any field directive or verbal change that hasn't been formally approved as a
          <a href="/features/change-orders/">change order</a> is at risk at closeout. GCs are less inclined
          to approve additional scope after the work is done and the budget is set. Resolve every outstanding
          change order — including any credits — before submitting the final pay application.
        </p>

        <h2>At substantial completion</h2>

        <h3>Certificate of Substantial Completion</h3>
        <p>
          Substantial completion is the contractual point at which the owner can use the finished
          work for its intended purpose, even if a punch list remains. It is typically documented
          with a Certificate of Substantial Completion — the AIA G704 form is the industry-standard
          template for this document. Make sure the certificate identifies your scope's outstanding
          items so what remains is unambiguous.
        </p>
        <p>
          The substantial completion date often starts the clock on warranty periods and can affect
          when liquidated damages stop running. Confirm the date in writing before signing anything.
          Do not sign a final unconditional lien waiver at this milestone — that exchange belongs
          with final payment, after the closeout package is accepted.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose rounded-2xl border border-brand-100 bg-brand-50 p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900">
            Put the date, the punch list and the retainage on one signed page
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600">
            Our free <a
              href="/templates/certificate-of-completion/"
              class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium"
              >certificate of substantial completion template</a
            > fixes the completion date, the warranty start and the retainage release, and the
            <a
              href="/templates/punch-list/"
              class="text-brand-600 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium">punch list template</a
            > is the list you attach to it. Fillable PDF and Word, no email required.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2>Final pay application and retainage</h2>
        <p>
          Once the GC accepts your closeout package, submit your final
          <a href="/features/pay-applications/">pay application</a> billing the remaining contract balance
          — including the retainage line — to bring the schedule of values to 100% complete. Retainage
          is commonly 5–10% of the contract value held back throughout the project; the final pay application
          is where you bill it. For the G702/G703 line-by-line, use the
          <a href="/blog/aia-pay-application-guide/">AIA pay application guide</a>.
        </p>
        <h3>Final lien waivers</h3>
        <p>
          At final payment, you'll sign a final lien waiver releasing your right to place a
          mechanic's lien against the property for the work you performed. Keep it conditional until
          funds clear, then exchange the unconditional. You'll also need final waivers from any
          sub-tier subcontractors or material suppliers who furnished labor or materials to your
          scope — a missing supplier waiver can hold the entire project's closeout. Our
          <a href="/blog/construction-lien-waivers-explained/">lien waiver guide</a> covers the four waiver
          types and what to verify before signing a final unconditional.
        </p>
        <p>
          Stay in contact with your GC after submission to track where payment is in the chain.
          Retainage can sit in a GC's account for weeks if no one is following up on it. Know your
          contract's release conditions and ask for specifics — what milestone triggers the release,
          and what the GC's payment cycle is after they receive funds from the owner. Our
          <a href="/blog/retainage-construction-guide/">retainage guide</a> covers rates, step-downs,
          and how to track withheld funds across jobs.
        </p>

        <h2>Track closeout in your project phases</h2>
        <p>
          Closeout involves too many moving parts to manage from memory or a shared inbox. In
          BuildWorkPro, the
          <a href="/features/project-management/">project management</a> module lets you build a dedicated
          closeout phase directly inside the project — with tasks for each document, each inspection,
          each waiver — and assign each item to the team member responsible for getting it done. That
          is the closeout checklist as project management: one phase, owners, dates, and a Gantt view
          of what is blocking final payment.
        </p>
      </div>

